## Claim
Text: "Previous flu vaccines may have made President Donald Trump susceptible to COVID-19"
Claim author: Miriam Fauzia
Claim date: October 21, 2020

## Initial Q&A
### Did President Donald Trump receive any influenza (flu) vaccines in the years or months leading up to his positive COVID-19 diagnosis in October 2020?
Answer: Yes, President Donald Trump received flu vaccines in the years leading up to his positive COVID-19 diagnosis in October 2020. As reported in the source, Trump referenced his own flu vaccination history in an Aug. 31, 2020, interview, with the article noting, "Did Trump's previous three flu vaccines make him more susceptible to contracting CVD?"

Source URL: https://www.usatoday.com/story/news/factcheck/2020/10/21/fact-check-donald-trumps-flu-shots-covid-risk-arent-linked/3623699001/

### Is there peer-reviewed scientific evidence demonstrating that seasonal flu vaccines increase a person's susceptibility to SARS-CoV-2 infection or COVID-19 disease?
Answer: The search result does not contain peer-reviewed evidence directly linking seasonal flu vaccines to increased susceptibility to SARS-CoV-2 or COVID-19, as the cited source is a non-peer-reviewed rapid response comment and references a 2012 study on coronaviruses in general, not SARS-CoV-2 specifically.

Source URL: https://www.bmj.com/content/368/bmj.m810/rr-0

### What specific immunological mechanism has been proposed by the author or cited researchers by which prior flu vaccination could make someone more susceptible to COVID-19?
Answer: The proposed specific immunological mechanism is "virus interference," whereby a flu vaccination might alter the immune system's response in a way that increases susceptibility to other respiratory viruses, as referenced in the misinterpretation of a U.S. Department of Defense study by anti-vaccine advocates.

Source URL: https://www.snopes.com/fact-check/flu-vaccine-covid-buttar/

### What do published epidemiological studies say about the relationship between seasonal influenza vaccination and the risk of contracting COVID-19?
Answer: The cited search result does not contain sufficient information to answer the question about published epidemiological studies on the relationship between seasonal influenza vaccination and the risk of contracting COVID-19, as it is a rapid response letter referencing a 2012 study on influenza vaccination and other respiratory viruses in children, not peer-reviewed epidemiological evidence specific to COVID-19. Therefore, none.

Source URL: https://www.bmj.com/content/368/bmj.m810/rr-0

### Has any prominent immunologist or virologist publicly supported the hypothesis that flu vaccines can increase susceptibility to SARS-CoV-2?
Answer: Based on this source, no prominent immunologist or virologist is cited as supporting the hypothesis that flu vaccines increase susceptibility to SARS-CoV-2; the claims primarily originate from misinterpretations of a Department of Defense study and from anti-vaccine advocates like Dr. Rashid Buttar, who the article identifies as an osteopath and conspiracy theorist, not a recognized authority in immunology or virology.

Source URL: https://www.snopes.com/fact-check/flu-vaccine-covid-buttar/

### What evidence or sources did author Miriam Fauzia cite in the October 21, 2020 article to support the claim that flu vaccines may have contributed to Donald Trump's COVID-19 infection?
Answer: In her article, Miriam Fauzia cited an Instagram post from the account @holistichealthandmore that referenced an August 31, 2020, Fox News interview where President Trump mentioned his flu vaccination history, as well as two scientific papers: a 2012 study in *Clinical Infectious Diseases* and a January 2020 study by Greg Wolff published in *Vaccine*. However, the USA TODAY fact-check clarifies that these studies do not support the claim, as they did not involve COVID-19 and the latter author explicitly warned against misinterpreting his findings.

Source URL: https://www.usatoday.com/story/news/factcheck/2020/10/21/fact-check-donald-trumps-flu-shots-covid-risk-arent-linked/3623699001/

### Are there known cases or studies of negative immune interference (such as antibody-dependent enhancement or original antigenic sin) between influenza vaccines and coronaviruses?
Answer: Based on the search result, there is no strong scientific evidence supporting negative immune interference between influenza vaccines and coronaviruses like SARS-CoV-2. While one 2012 study suggested a possible link, subsequent studies and a review by the CDC found that "the preponderance of evidence suggests that this is not a common or regular occurrence and that influenza vaccination does not, in fact, make people more susceptible to other respiratory infections." The often-cited Department of Defense study did not involve SARS-CoV-2 and its findings were misinterpreted by anti-vaccine advocates.

Source URL: https://www.snopes.com/fact-check/flu-vaccine-covid-buttar/

### What alternative explanations exist for Donald Trump's susceptibility to COVID-19 that do not involve prior flu vaccination?
Answer: The search result, while from 2016, provides relevant alternative risk factors for Trump's COVID-19 susceptibility unrelated to flu vaccination. At the time, Trump was 70 years old, weighed 236 pounds at a height of 6'3"—placing him "just shy of being obese"—and was taking the cholesterol drug Crestor, indicating elevated cholesterol levels. Age and obesity are well-established risk factors for severe COVID-19 outcomes, and these physiological characteristics would have been even more pronounced by 2020, when he was 74 years old. These factors offer plausible, evidence-based alternative explanations for his vulnerability to the virus that have nothing to do with prior flu vaccination.

Source URL: https://www.npr.org/2016/09/15/494081537/trump-releases-weight-cholesterol-blood-sugar-and-other-medical-information

### Have public health agencies such as the CDC or WHO issued any statements or guidance regarding a potential link between flu vaccination and increased COVID-19 risk?
Answer: Yes, public health agencies have explicitly addressed and dismissed this claim. The CDC director Robert Redfield emphasized the flu vaccine's importance, stating it "may allow there to be a hospital bed available for your mother or grandmother that may get coronavirus." Furthermore, the Military Health System clarified that its study, often misinterpreted to suggest a link, "does not show or suggest that influenza vaccination predisposes in any way, the potential for infection with the more severe forms of coronavirus, such as COVID-19." The article concludes that experts and officials advise continued flu vaccination, as "evidence of virus interference... is weak and inconclusive."

Source URL: https://www.factcheck.org/2020/04/no-evidence-that-flu-shot-increases-risk-of-covid-19/

### Was the claim presented by Miriam Fauzia framed as established science, a hypothesis, or speculation, and does the article's evidence support the conclusion as stated?
Answer: The claim was framed as speculation, using the word "may," but the article's evidence does not support the conclusion; in fact, the cited studies were unrelated to COVID-19, and a recent study found "no link between the flu vaccine and COVID-19 susceptibility."

Source URL: https://www.usatoday.com/story/news/factcheck/2020/10/21/fact-check-donald-trumps-flu-shots-covid-risk-arent-linked/3623699001/

## Final Judgement
The fact-check reveals that while President Trump did receive flu vaccines prior to his COVID-19 diagnosis, the specific claim that this made him susceptible is unsupported. The evidence shows that the studies cited in the original article were either not about COVID-19 or were misinterpreted, and the proposed "virus interference" mechanism is weak and inconclusive. Public health agencies have explicitly dismissed this link, and alternative, well-established risk factors for severe COVID-19 (such as age and obesity) are present.

The information needed to finalize the check—specifically, credible scientific evidence linking flu vaccines to increased susceptibility to SARS-CoV-2—is lacking from the record. The available evidence actively refutes the claim rather than merely failing to support it.

Based on the recorded evidence, which explicitly shows the cited studies were not relevant to COVID-19 and that experts and agencies have rejected the claimed connection, the conclusion is that the claim is incorrect.

`refuted`

### Verdict: REFUTED

### Justification
The fact-check establishes that while Donald Trump did receive flu vaccines prior to his COVID-19 diagnosis, the cited studies [do not support](https://www.usatoday.com/story/news/factcheck/2020/10/21/fact-check-donald-trumps-flu-shots-covid-risk-arent-linked/3623699001/) the alleged link, as they were unrelated to SARS-CoV-2 or were misinterpreted. The proposed "virus interference" mechanism is [weak and inconclusive](https://www.snopes.com/fact-check/flu-vaccine-covid-buttar/), and public health agencies have [explicitly dismissed the claim](https://www.factcheck.org/2020/04/no-evidence-that-flu-shot-increases-risk-of-covid-19/), emphasizing established risk factors like [age and obesity](https://www.npr.org/2016/09/15/494081537/trump-releases-weight-cholesterol-blood-sugar-and-other-medical-information) instead.